# Hub — Testing Authority

## 1. Canonical ownership and topology

The founder greenfield cutover is authoritative. Preserve these boundaries:

| Stage | CI job | Canonical location | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| unit | `backend-unit` | `backend/tests/{domain}/` | genuine isolated Go units only |
| unit | `frontend-unit` | `frontend/tests/{domain}/`, `frontend/tests/support/` | genuine Vitest/RTL units only |
| contract | `python-contract` | `testframework/tests/contract/` | API, schema, event, and source contracts |
| API | `python-api` | `testframework/tests/api/` | public backend and frontend-BFF behavior |
| integration | `python-integration` | `testframework/tests/integration/` | DB, broker, and storage boundaries |
| e2e | `python-e2e-smoke`, `python-e2e-full` | `testframework/tests/e2e/` | end-to-end user behavior |
| load | `python-load` | `testframework/load/` | Locust profiles |

`testframework/src/hub_testkit/**` is the only reusable Python package root.
Executable Python SUT suites belong only in
`testframework/tests/{contract,api,integration,e2e}/**`. Do not recreate an
obsolete top-level suite tree such as `core/`, `contract/`, `api/`,
`integration/`, or `e2e/` outside that canonical `tests/` root.

The following remain separate roots and must not be mixed into package code or
SUT suites: `testframework/selftests/`, `testframework/cases/`,
`testframework/schemas/`, `testframework/policies/`, `testframework/load/`,
`testframework/docker/`, `testframework/runner/`, and
`testframework/reports/`. Their documented roles remain distinct.

`testframework/pyproject.toml`, `testframework/uv.lock`, and
`testframework/src/hub_testkit/py.typed` are future `NOT_READY` items. The
package bootstrap, target suites, and outer launcher are likewise `NOT_READY`
until independently implemented and accepted. A directory, a document, or a
static check is not runtime evidence.

Only proved genuine Go and frontend units may move to their canonical unit
roots. Legacy Go component/API/integration tests, static-contract Vitest,
TypeScript Playwright, and their support, configuration, and entrypoints are
deleted by exact manifest and are not ported. They are never a fallback for the
greenfield Python non-unit framework.

## 2. Test design and suite selection

Select a suite from the behavior's risk, boundary, and feedback needs, not
from source-file geometry or a coverage quota. Each applicable requirement is
traceable to task, requirement, and TC identifiers with an observable oracle,
forbidden side effects, preconditions, data, and cleanup.

Consider relevant happy and invalid paths; authentication, authorization, and
tenant isolation; empty/one/many and boundary values; null, Unicode, time and
timezone; duplicates, ordering and compatibility; timeout, outage, partial
completion, retry and recovery; cancellation, concurrency, replay and
idempotency; resource pressure; and applicable security, privacy, accessibility
and performance risks. Mark an inapplicable family `N/A` with a short reason.

One test expresses one behavioral concept. Prefer DAMP scenarios and DRY
mechanical setup. Assert observable behavior rather than incidental private
call ordering. UI tests start from accessible semantic locators; fixed sleeps
do not synchronize a test. Shared mutable accounts, records, schema, artifacts,
ports, or namespaces are forbidden: identify task, run, and worker ownership.

When a test fails, perform `/test-triage` before remediation. Classify the
symptom exactly once as `ROOT=code|test|infra|flaky|stop`, verify expected
behavior against authoritative documentation, then repair and rerun the
applicable regression through the canonical gate. Do not alter an assertion
merely to make the run green.

## 3. E2E semantics

`smoke` is a pytest marker for the smoke subset. `full` is an outer-launcher
profile that selects the complete E2E suite; it is not a pytest marker. A
missing scaffold, job, or launcher remains `NOT_READY`; in that condition no
substitute runner, legacy test suite, host command, or direct container command
may claim that the profile ran.

## 4. Canonical execution and cleanup

Windows-host use is limited to reading or editing files and Git operations.
Tests, validators, linters, builds, vet, Vite, backend or frontend test
processes, pytest, Playwright, and Locust execute only through the future
versioned outer launcher API owned by `testframework/runner/**` and invoked by
`/qa-gate` once it is implemented and independently verified.

The launcher must create task-owned execution with explicit CPU, memory, PID,
and wall-clock limits; isolated data, ports, network, and artifacts; and an
owner-marked `testframework/reports/<task-run>/` location. Before execution it
records the exact task-owned resource IDs it may clean. Its one unconditional
outer finalizer cleans only those pre-recorded exact IDs, then proves their
exact absence. Never use pattern, label, project-wide, or otherwise broad
cleanup as a substitute for this contract.

`testframework/docker/**` holds immutable execution assets and contracts only;
it does not own launcher code or an alternate entrypoint. Direct host
test/build/validator commands and direct Docker or Compose invocations are not
gates, fallbacks, or evidence. If the canonical launcher is absent, the
launcher is `NOT_READY` and runtime is `NOT_RUN`.

## 5. Evidence and acceptance

Runnable evidence, once canonical execution exists, records the task/run,
source revision, immutable image identity, versioned launcher invocation and
recorded inner command, resource limits, selected service profile, collection
and result counts, duration, owner-marked artifact location, pre-recorded
resource IDs, and exact-absence cleanup proof. Zero collection, unexpected
skips, leaked resources, or an unavailable canonical execution are not a pass.

High-risk behavior requires a meaningful negative sentinel or mutation showing
that the protected behavior would fail without its protection. File presence,
raw searches, mock-only green assertions, coverage percentages, and historical
host-run output are not behavior evidence.

For this documentation-only authority wave, runtime remains `NOT_RUN` and no
package, launcher, target suite, test, validator, build, Docker action, or
runtime claim is authorized.
